Sure! Here's a simple function that does just that:

```javascript
async function fetchData(apiUrl) {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(apiUrl);
        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
        }
        const data = await response.json();
        return data;
    } catch (error) {
        console.error('There was a problem with the fetch operation:', error);
    }
}
```

This function uses the Fetch API to get data from the specified `apiUrl`. Make sure to handle any errors that may arise, as users may unintentionally provide incorrect URLs or other issues. If you need further modifications for specific usage, let me know!

Certainly! Lazy loading images in a React application can be done using the `loading` attribute for the `<img>` tag or by using a library like `react-lazyload`. Here’s a simple example using the built-in attribute:

```jsx
const LazyImage = ({ src, alt }) => {
    return <img src={src} alt={alt} loading="lazy" />;
};
```

This will defer loading the image until it reaches the viewport, which improves performance. If you need a more advanced solution, let me know and I can provide an example using `react-lazyload`!
